FAQ = [
 ("Do 2-door Wrangler JL side steps fit the 4-door Unlimited?",
  "No. Side steps run between the wheel wells, and the 4-door Unlimited has a 118.4 in wheelbase against 96.8 in for the 2-door, per Wikipedia's JL page. That is more than 21 in of extra rocker, so a 2-door step is far too short and its brackets land in the wrong places. Every maker sells separate parts: Westin's HDX Drop is 56-14055 for the 2-door and 56-140652 for the Unlimited, and Rough Country's listings name 2-Door or 4-Door in the title. Count your doors before you order anything on this page."),
 ("Do JK Wrangler running boards fit a JL?",
  "Treat them as different vehicles. The JL replaced the JK for the 2018 model year with a new body, and Westin lists separate HDX Drop parts for the 2007–2018 JK Unlimited (56-13295) and the JL Unlimited (56-14065 and 56-140652). Because 2018 was a model year for both the JK and the JL, a listing that says only '2018 Wrangler' can mean either one. Buy a part whose title says JL, or names 2019 or later, and skip anything that names the JK unless the seller confirms it also fits the JL."),
 ("Does my Wrangler already have factory rock rails or side steps?",
  "Look under the doors. We could not open a Jeep page that lists factory rock rails or tube steps by trim, so we will not state which trims have them. Owners on JLwranglerforums describe factory rock rails as standard on the Rubicon and discuss add-on steps that mount to those rails, such as the MORE Hide-A-Step, without removing them. If your Jeep has factory rails or steps, an aftermarket step from this page replaces them or shares their mounting points, so ask the seller whether the kit is designed to go on with the factory rails removed or alongside them."),
 ("Are power steps worth it on a Wrangler?",
  "They solve the Wrangler's two competing needs. A fixed step hangs below the rocker all the time, which costs ground clearance and catches rocks. A power step drops when a door opens and folds up against the body when it closes, so the Jeep keeps its clearance on the trail and still gives a low step in the driveway. The cost is money and wiring. RealTruck's AMP Research page lists PowerStep models from $1,249.99 to $2,299.99, and Rough Country's dual-motor kit is the lower-priced brand-name route. If the Jeep never leaves pavement, a fixed drop step does the same job for a fraction of the price."),
 ("What is the difference between a drop step, a tube step and a rock slider?",
  "A tube step, or nerf bar, is a round or oval tube that runs along the rocker with step pads on top; it sits close to the body and keeps the most clearance of the fixed types. A drop step bends the tube down at each door so the step pad sits lower, which helps on a stock or lifted Jeep with a high sill. A rock slider is built to take the weight of the Jeep on a rock, so it mounts to the frame or body with heavier brackets and often has a flat top you can step on. Rough Country's Contoured Drop Steps sit between the types: flush rails with a step at each door."),
 ("Do these steps fit the Wrangler 4xe and Rubicon 392?",
  "None of the listing titles on this page excludes the 4xe or the 392, and none names them either. The 4xe carries its battery pack under the rear seat and its charge port on the front fender, which does not touch the rocker, but the makers we read do not say so. The 392 has its own exhaust and body cladding. Before you order for either one, ask the seller to confirm the part number against your VIN. Our floor liner guide covers the 4xe's rear floor difference, which is a separate issue."),
 ("Do I need to drill to install Wrangler side steps?",
  "Usually not. Westin says the HDX Drop is a bolt-on installation with no drilling required for most applications and ships with a vehicle-specific mount kit. Rough Country's 2-door nerf step listing says bolt-on install. Rock sliders are the exception: some use the body mounting points, others add frame brackets, and a few need holes drilled or nutserts set, so read the instructions for the slider you choose before the Jeep goes on jack stands. Power steps add a wiring job on top of the brackets."),
 ("Will running boards hurt my Wrangler's off-road clearance?",
  "Yes, by the amount they hang below the rocker. A drop step is the lowest fixed type because the step pad sits well under the sill. A tube step or a slider with a step keeps more, and a power step keeps all of it when the doors are closed. Approach and departure angles are set by the bumpers, so steps do not change those, but breakover and side clearance both drop. For a Jeep that sees rocks, choose a rock slider rated for impacts or a power step that tucks away."),
 ("Can I use Westin's HDX Drop on a Rubicon with the factory rails?",
  "Westin's page we read does not say, and the Amazon titles for 56-14055 and 56-140652 name the JL 2-door and Unlimited without a trim. Owners on JLwranglerforums report that steps made for the factory Rubicon rails mount to the rails themselves, while rocker-mounted steps from other makers go on in place of them. Ask Westin or the seller whether the HDX Drop brackets clear or replace the factory rails on your trim before you buy."),
 ("How much weight can these steps hold?",
  "We did not read a published load rating for any of the seven picks. Westin's HDX Drop page describes a solid one-piece bar with heavy-duty die-stamped foot plates but gives no figure. The power step listings describe dual motors but no rating. Ask the seller for a per-side rating before you buy if a heavy rider or a loaded cooler will stand on the step, and never use a running board as a jacking point. Rock sliders are the only type on this page built to carry the Jeep's weight, and only the frame-mounted ones."),
]

